-- title:
--   Weighted l2-sums of levels
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that a real Banach space $X$ with the approximation property is K-convex (the Rademacher projection is bounded on $L_2(\{\pm1\}^n;X)$ uniformly in $n$) if and only if $X$ and its dual $X^*$ both have finite cotype. The converse direction shows that such $X$ has nontrivial type, i.e. does not contain $\ell_1^n$ uniformly, and then derives K-convexity from a power-gap estimate for commuting coordinate projections. Let $A_0,A_1,\dots$ be subspaces of $X$.
--
--   1. **Level sum.** $\ell_2$-sum $\bigl(\bigoplus_lA_l\bigr)_{\ell_2}$.
--   2. **Level terms.** $z\mapsto2^{-l}z_l$, the weighted coordinate maps into $X$.
--   3. **Weighted map.**
--   $$
--   J(z)=\sum_l2^{-l}z_l ,
--   $$
--   a bounded operator into $X$. If the $A_l$ are finite-dimensional, $J$ is a norm limit of finite-rank maps, hence compact.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Analysis/Cotype/WeightedCompact.lean

